  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 180d0828f9cf423459360235cd6922f2, type: 3}
  m_Name: Guts
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::DeepVisionTutorialSO
  steps:
  - The canine intestines form the major digestive and absorptive portion of the
    gastrointestinal tract. They extend throughout the abdominal cavity and are divided
    into small and large intestinal segments.
  - The small intestine includes the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum, where most nutrient
    digestion and absorption occur. Digestive enzymes and bile interact with ingested
    food within this region.
  - Finger-like villi and microscopic microvilli line the intestinal wall, dramatically
    increasing absorptive surface area. Nutrients pass through these structures into
    blood and lymphatic circulation.
  - The large intestine absorbs water and electrolytes while compacting undigested
    material into feces. It also contains beneficial microbial populations involved
    in fermentation and vitamin synthesis.
  - Peristaltic contractions generated by smooth muscle layers move digestive contents
    progressively through the intestinal tract. Coordinated motility ensures proper
    digestion, absorption, and waste elimination.
